  "query_preview": "..\n\nA surface ridge axis located across the upper Ohio Valley and \ncentral Appalachians will sustain a south-southwest gradient across \nSe Mi today. This will result in breezy afternoon conditions, \nespecially across the Saginaw Valley and thumb where 25 to 30 knots \nare shown in the mixed layer on model soundings. This will in turn \ndrive afternoon temperatures in the low 60s. \n\nLow to mid level anticyclonic flow will remain dominate across much \nof the eastern US for the next several days. The corresponding \nregion of subsidence will sustain abundant dry air across much of \nthe eastern US, including the Great Lakes, through at least the \nmiddle of the week. This along with a weakening sfc gradient will \nsupport a relatively high diurnal temperature response through next \nweek, with persistent highs in the 60s and nighttime lows in the 30s \nand 40s. Rising geopotential heights across the Upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes Monday into Tuesday will cause sfc pressure rises across \nnortheast Ontario and nrn Quebec, leading to the center of sfc high \npressure becoming reoriented northeast of the Great Lakes region. \nThis will result in light east-norheasterly winds off the lakes mid \nto late in the week, possibly setting the stage for some episodes of \nfog. Overall, the extensive dry airmass across the eastern US and \nthermal profiles which will inhibit lake clouds will lead to \ncontinued clear skies into at least Wednesday.",
